+/// Admissible upper bound on [`word_count_parsimony`] for every word count
+/// a path could still finish at.
+///
+/// A path that has taken `words` words and has `still_possible` IPA
+/// characters left ends at some count in `[words, words + still_possible]`
+/// — at least `words`, and at most one word per remaining character,
+/// because every word consumes at least one.  Write `f(c)` for
+/// `word_count_parsimony(c, t)`: for `c <= t` it is `c / t`, rising in
+/// `c`, and for `c >= t` it is `t / c`, falling in `c`.  So `f` is
+/// unimodal in `c` with its single maximum at `c = t`, and the maximum of
+/// `f` over any interval is `f` evaluated at `t` clamped into that
+/// interval.
+///
+/// The interval is used as it is, including the empty path's `words = 0`:
+/// `f` is monotone on each side of `t` there too, so the clamp is still
+/// the exact maximum, and flooring the interval at one would have made
+/// the bound *larger* than the truth for a path that has not yet taken a
+/// word — padding, in the one case where the search uses the key at its
+/// most.
+///
+/// The bound is therefore not merely *loose enough* to keep
+/// `structural_bounds_dominate_the_real_scorer` true: it is the largest
+/// value `f` can possibly take over the reachable set, so the axis's
+/// contribution to the admissible key is exact wherever the target's own
+/// word count is still reachable and is the correct endpoint relaxation
+/// where it is not.  A bound that was merely padded — say, pinned at 1.0
+/// — would leave the fence green and the search pruning strictly worse
+/// candidates than it needed to.
+fn word_count_parsimony_upper_bound(
+    words: usize,
+    target_words: usize,
+    still_possible: usize,
+) -> f64 {
+    let target = target_words.max(1);
+    let lo = words;
+    let hi = words.saturating_add(still_possible);
+    word_count_parsimony(target.clamp(lo, hi), target)
+}
